Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::string line;
    std::istringstream iss(markdown);

    while (std::getline(iss, line)) {
        html += "<p>" + line + "</p>";
    }

    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a sample markdown text.\nThis is another line.\nAnd a third line.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function takes a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text as input and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. It uses a `std::istringstream` to read the markdown text line by line and wraps each line in a `<p>` tag to create the HTML.

Note that this implementation assumes that the markdown text is formatted as plain text with line breaks. If you need to support more complex markdown features, such as headers or lists, you may need to use a more sophisticated markdown parser library.

